{"name":"Head Over Heels","hex":"#605972","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"head over heels","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Tumbling upside down; somersaulting.","At top speed; frantically."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Hopelessly smitten; madly in love."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/head_over_heels"}],"entries":[{"title":"Head over Heels (1922 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Head over Heels is a 1922 American comedy film starring Mabel Normand and directed by Paul Bern and Victor Schertzinger. This is a surviving comedy film at the Library of Congress. The supporting cast includes Raymond Hatton and Adolphe Menjou."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Head_Over_Heels_%281922%29_-_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_over_Heels_(1922_film)","wikidata":"Q3783864"},{"title":"Head over Heels (1937 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Head over Heels is a 1937 British musical film directed by Sonnie Hale and starring Jessie Matthews, Robert Flemyng and Louis Borel. It was written by Marjorie Gaffney, Fred Thompson and Dwight Taylor, adapted from the 1931 play Pierre ou Jack by Francis de Croisset."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d5/Head_over_Heels_%281937_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_over_Heels_(1937_film)","wikidata":"Q5689449"},{"title":"Head over Heels (Tears for Fears song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Head over Heels\" is a song recorded by the English new wave band Tears for Fears for their second studio album Songs from the Big Chair (1985). The song was released by Phonogram Records and Mercury Records, as the album's fourth single on 14 June 1985 in the UK. It was the band's tenth single release in the United Kingdom and eighth top 40 hit in the country, peaking at number 12. In the United States, it was released in September 1985 as the third single from the album and continued the band's run of hits there, peaking at number three on the Billboard Hot 100 chart. In addition to the regular 7\" and 12\" formats, a 10\" single and limited edition four-leaf-clover-shaped picture disc were also issued for the single's release in the UK. The song was also an international success, reaching the top 40 in several countries."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/90/TFF_Head_Over_Heels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_over_Heels_(Tears_for_Fears_song)","wikidata":"Q3783876"},{"title":"Head","blocks":[{"text":"A head is the part of an organism which usually includes the ears, brain, forehead, cheeks, chin, eyes, nose, and mouth, each of which aids in various sensory functions such as sight, hearing, smell, and taste. Some very simple animals may not have a head, but many bilaterally symmetric forms do, regardless of size."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Portrait_of_a_Meerkat.jpg/330px-Portrait_of_a_Meerkat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head","wikidata":"Q23640"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Beethovendeathmask.jpg/330px-Beethovendeathmask.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Beethovendeathmask.jpg/330px-Beethovendeathmask.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The head of a death mask","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeethovendeathmask.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/View_of_a_Skull_III.jpg/120px-View_of_a_Skull_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t